\label{equi_discrete}
By discretizing the stochastic Hamilton's principle, we have obtained the Type-I integrator (and its variants) in Section~\ref{d_ham}. On the other hand, by discretizing the stochastic Hamilton-Pontryagin principle, we have obtained the Type-II integrator in Section~\ref{d_hph}. It is natural to consider the relation between the obtained integrators. In fact, comparing the stochastic integrators in Theorem~\ref{Theorem_App_Int} and \ref{Theorem_Type_II} term by term, one finds that they are equivalent if and only if
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta g}\left(g^{n}_{j},\mu^{n+1/2}_{j}\right) = 0
\qquad
\text{and}
\qquad
\frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta \mu}\left(g^{n}_{j},\mu^{n+1/2}_{j}\right)
= \Theta^{n+1/2}_{j},
\end{equation*}
which is equivalent to 
\begin{equation*}
    \Phi\left(g^{n}_{j},\mu^{n+1/2}_{j}\right) = \left<\mu^{n+1/2}_{j},\Theta^{n+1/2}_{j}\right> + C,
\end{equation*}
where $C\in\mathbb{R}$ is a constant, which is a discrete counterpart of the equivalence condition \eqref{equivalence}.

It follows from \eqref{dis_kin_eq} that
\begin{align*}
\Delta t\zeta^{n+1/2}_{j} + \Theta^{n+1/2}_{j}
\left(W^{(M)}_{n+1,j} - W^{(M)}_{n,j}\right)
=
\tau^{-1}\left(\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}\right).
\end{align*}
Taking variations on both sides and using the definition of the right trivialized derivative yield
\begin{align*}
\Delta t\zeta^{n+1/2}_{j}
&=
\delta \left[
\tau^{-1}\left(\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}\right)
\right]  \\
&= D\tau^{-1}\left(\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}\right)\cdot
\delta\left(\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}\right)  \\
&= \mathrm{d}_{\tau^{-1}\left(\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}\right)}\tau^{-1}
\left(
\delta\left(\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}\right)\cdot
\left(g^{n+1}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n}_{j}
\right),
\end{align*}
with
\begin{align*}
\delta\left(\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}\right)\cdot
\left(g^{n+1}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n}_{j}
&= - \left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}\delta g^{n}_{j}
+ \mathrm{Ad}^{*}_{\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}}\left(g^{n+1}_{j}\right)^{-1}\delta g^{n+1}_{j}  \\
&= - \eta^{n}_{j}
+ \mathrm{Ad}^{*}_{\left(g^{n}_{j}\right)^{-1}g^{n+1}_{j}}\eta^{n+1}_{j},
\end{align*}
where the last equality follows from the definition of $\eta^{n}_{j}$. Discrete variation \eqref{var_gamma_2} can be proved in a similar way.

